analysis.originalLogic:
CopyProp's SSA copy-class computation unifies locals connected by copy assignments (`_1 = copy _2`, etc.), then replaces uses with the class representative. The pre-existing guard was:

    // Do not unify two borrowed locals.
    if borrowed_classes.contains(local) && borrowed_classes.contains(head) {
        continue;
    }

So it skipped unification only when BOTH the local being assigned and the source/head were in `borrowed_classes` (address-taken). If only one side was borrowed, the two locals were still merged into one copy class and their uses rewritten.

The PR description states the intent plainly: "Do not unify borrowed locals in CopyProp." and "Instead of trying yet another scheme to unify borrowed locals in CopyProp, let's just stop trying. We had already enough miscompilations because of this." It "Fixes https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/143491".

analysis.incorrectTransformation:
Historically incorrect transformation: under the old `&&` guard, when exactly one of `local`/`head` was borrowed (address-taken), CopyProp still merged them, propagating uses of one local to the other and eliminating the copy. That is wrong when a borrowed local may be written through its reference before the copied value is read again: the merge erases the distinction between the value at copy time and the value after the intervening write, so a later read can observe the wrong value (miscompilation). The corrected transformation changes the guard to `||`, so no unification occurs whenever EITHER side is borrowed:

    -        // Do not unify two borrowed locals.
    -        if borrowed_classes.contains(local) && borrowed_classes.contains(head) {
    +        // Do not unify borrowed locals.
    +        if borrowed_classes.contains(local) || borrowed_classes.contains(head) {
                 continue;
             }

This is a conservative restriction of the optimization, not a new rewrite. The diff-confirmed effect is that copy assignments are retained, e.g. `_2 = copy _1` stays (borrowed.rs) and `_4 = copy _5` stays with the later call reading `_4` instead of `_5` (write_to_borrowed.rs).
